00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.590 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilshire host and our guest is  Priya Natarajan a professor in the  department's of astronomy and physics at  Yale University professor Natarajan is a  theoretical astrophysicist interested in  cosmology gravitational lensing and  black hole physics her research involves  mapping the detailed distribution of  dark matter in the universe exploiting  the bending of light and route to us  from distant galaxies however we are not  here today to talk to professor in at  origin about astronomy and physics we  are going to talk with her about gender  parity issues  professor Natarajan is the current chair  of the women faculty forum at Yale and  she recently co-organized along with  Judith Resnik and Reva Siegel at the  Yale Law School the very first gruber  conference titled parity as practice the  politics of equality  welcome professor nada Rajan thank you  very much what led you to organize this  conference why do you think it is  important to have done it well first of  all it was a unique opportunity to kick  off sort of the Gruber fellowship  program moving to Yale and there seem to  be a real confluence with the goals that  the wff exemplifies in terms of exchange  of women's Scholars on issues pertinent  to women's scholarship by women on women  and also trying to understand the role  of gender the lens of gender and its and  the role that it plays in society in  many different domains so it was a lucky  coincidence that the remit of the wff  coincided with what the Gruber program  really stands for as well so we decided  to have this interdisciplinary  conference sort of an unprecedented one  really kick off a very new and important  and potentially powerful conversation  and what were some of the issues or  areas that you examines during the  conference so the notion of gender  parity is understood quite differently  in different domains okay explain that  so for example and you know understood  differently in different domains in the  public sphere in the political sphere in  universities and in the domestic sphere  and of course on top of that layering  there's a real difference in the  understanding which is culturally based  so across the world there are very  different notions of what gender parity  means and what it might look like so we  started off the conference really trying  to examine what the differences and the  similarities are in our notions of  parity for all of us situated in very  different places in the university my  particular interest is of course looking  at women in universities and in academia  in particular trying to understand what  are sort of the systemic difficulties  that women might face and how  universities what they could actually do  to ensure gender parity and sort of a  more level playing field but we examined  many different domains so we looked at  the political sphere looking at the  representation of women you know there  are some countries in the world in the  u.s. we don't have parity laws but in  many countries of the world Spain France  India Norway and Turkey they're  experimenting legally with set asides  and quotas and so we looked at that we  looked at the experience of how useful  they have been in places and how they  worked we looked at the corporate world  where again in some Nordic countries  there are requirements that there should  be 40 percent of the board of a  corporate board should be women you know  that's been in place for a short time  but we wanted to examine how that's  worked and whether that's an interesting  and useful intervention curious it's 40  percent not 50 percent that's right and  and then we looked at the domestic  sphere and you know and that's a very  fraught sphere because in a way  underlying sort of  the basis of parity is sort of how it's  played out in the domestic sphere  because you know it's the amount of work  that household work that and childcare  and nurturance that women do women tend  to do a larger share of it across the  board across cultures and that impacts  their participation in other spheres and  so then we looked at the university  sphere we looked at academia around the  world and then we also looked at gender  parity as it plays out in the  development agenda in the agenda of the  dealing with poverty dealing with women  in extremists so you know we covered a  huge sort of range and it was sort of  intentional because we wanted to learn  from practices so there are many  different meanings that people have  adopted for parity in some places gender  parity is parsed as gender equality and  in some places it is seen as under the  rubric of anti-discrimination so our  goal here was to sort of try and distill  practices and theoretical notions that  may translate across boundaries and that  we may actually find useful in our  respective spheres and both men and  women participated in the conference  absolutely but as you can probably  predict it was predominantly women  scholars it turns out and I think this  is something I really wish will change  hope I hope changes in my generation  that many of these issues actually of  gender parity are not women's issues  they're human issues and I'm really  hoping that more and more men will get  seriously drawn into this conversation  that would be very nice now in looking  at the world you touched on it briefly  how are we doing in terms of gender  parity out in the world I would imagine  some places are better than others but  is it is it is it more is it better or  is it worse percentage well you know  5050 you know country is kind of you  know moving in the right direction or is  it less than that well you know it's  very hard because then there are various  sieze along which you could measure  gender parity right and I think it would  be fair to say that it gender parity  remains an aspiration everywhere it's  not quite the reality and I think there  are a lot of contradictions right so you  may have countries where you've had a  woman head of state but doesn't doesn't  necessarily imply that the playing field  for a normal middle-class woman is any  more level or an impoverished woman is  any is in any better situation so I  think how we measure you know which  country is doing better on gender parity  issues is not so much a sort of a  national countrywide index but I think  along domains so we can see I think it  would be fair to say that women have  made great strides in all domains but  sadly there are still parts of the world  where personal safety violence against  women and sort of inequities and how the  law treats a man and a woman with  respect to marriage property etc are  still there in terms of the participants  in the conference I'm curious if and  talking about countries that have put  some kind of laws around gender issues  do people seem to think that that works  better that's more helpful towards  moving the the issue along so to speak  so it's very interesting that you ask  this because this formed one of the sort  of heated debates and the conference and  and the debate really rests around the  justification so we were trying to look  for so there's a rights theory  justification for why we need to be in a  just world and everyone needs to be  treated the same and that we form 50% of  population and so on but you know there  has been a very strong thread in society  where women have argued that on the  outcome of increased participation I  personally feel that you know there is a  justification for aspiring for gender  parity regardless of the outcome so I  think the question of whether having 40  percent women in power  and actually translates into being  better for women overall is not the  question to ask really it's hard to  assess and I think the report on that is  mixed for instance in local government  for example in India where they required  50% women some studies show that women  once they are elected into these  positions tend to focus on issues that  are tantamount and important to them  like water so water is an issue in India  and women often are carry water too for  their household so women in these local  governments tended to use start off with  water as a priority  however it's it's as I said it's mix  because if you look at the Nordic  countries what they found is when you  had 40 percent women in the national  legislature so we're talking about the  national level not the local level here  for the first six months or so we heard  reports that women focused on what we  would see as traditional sort of women's  agendas but then within six months there  was actually no difference and men and  women pushed very very similar agendas  so I think the question of whether the  outcomes are better for women is a  fraught question and I think what we  really need to focus on is that having a  more sort of even participation is good  for society as a whole  so what were some of the outcomes of the  conference so in one of the interesting  you know so first of all having a  conference like this with all these  different voices representing so many  exemplary scholars and activists was a  feat in and of itself and the fact that  we started a very important what we feel  is a very important interesting  conversation that can move forward that  can be helpful for people across domains  was one you know it was one in which we  are hoping to start new collaborations  intellectual collaborations as well as  learn sort of practical things from each  other so it was I think I see it as a  great success because it was the start  of a very novel conversation that hasn't  happened before and what do you think it  would take for  gender parity to actually happen and do  you think it's achievable well well I  hope it's achievable and I hope it's  achievable  during my lifetime but I think that the  notion used to be for example if you  look at the university's domain that you  know it's a question of time we just  don't have we have this metaphor of a  pipeline we just don't have enough women  coming through particularly in the STEM  fields the science technology and  mathematics engineering and mathematics  fields that there aren't enough women  who were starting off as undergraduate  so we don't have enough in the pool to  pick professors from and so on so forth  but it turns out that with all the work  and the awareness for the past 20 years  you know the pipeline is now populated  so it is no longer a question of just  time but you know we'll catch up in time  I think the things that really need work  on for achieving parity are the more in  insidious complicated subtle things and  they are sort of sort of the gender  stereotyping implicit absolutely  unconscious bias and so and I think what  we really need to think hard about for  every domain in society is what are the  little nudges that are needed to get  people to start examining their own  stereotypes men and women not just men  men and how do you facilitate those  nudges absolutely and how do you  incentivize them how do you first make  people aware of them and then how do you  incentivize people to actually change  and as you can imagine right in the  domain of science in STEM fields it's  particularly difficult because in these  disciplines which we all as  practitioners feel that we are objective  we are just and we are fair and that we  have very clear-cut criteria to  understand what is good scholarship and  what isn't you know it is not about  weighing one argument against the other  so you know it's a more so we feel and  so for us to accept that we have  unconscious biases is a huge challenge  but I think that's really where the  future a challenge is for for us of  course there are societies where the  challenges are more fundamental  with regard to sort of Human Rights  basic human rights but you know as I  said the challenges are very different  in very in in the various domains but I  think there's no escape that even once  we get the numbers up that there are  other more subtle barriers that we need  to tackle and that's the real challenge  for the future okay very good thank you  so much for being here today and sharing  some of the work that was done at the  conference thank you so much I'm happy  to be here for more information about  Professor Nataraja and her work please  visit our website at Yale edu backslash  Macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale

